    {
      "title": "Routing Tables and the Mental Model",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 0,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Routing Tables and the Mental Model",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. What a Routing Table Is",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Maps ==destination prefix== → **next hop** + **outgoing interface** (+ metric)",
                "Router looks up the destination ==IP==, {red:never the MAC}",
                "Route sources: {green:connected} (attached subnets), {blue:static} (admin-typed), {purple:dynamic} (OSPF/BGP/etc.)"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Hop-by-Hop Forwarding",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Each router decides ==independently== from its own table",
                "The packet's **IP stays the same** end to end",
                "The ==L2 header is rewritten== at every hop"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "kind": "flow",
              "spec": {
                "nodes": [
                  {
                    "text": "Host",
                    "sub": "dst IP fixed",
                    "ink": "green"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Router A",
                    "sub": "lookup, new L2",
                    "ink": "blue"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Router B",
                    "sub": "lookup, new L2",
                    "ink": "purple"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Destination",
                    "sub": "same IP",
                    "ink": "orange"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Choosing Between Routes",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Source",
                "Admin distance"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Connected",
                  "0"
                ],
                [
                  "Static",
                  "1"
                ],
                [
                  "eBGP",
                  "20"
                ],
                [
                  "OSPF",
                  "110"
                ],
                [
                  "RIP",
                  "120"
                ],
                [
                  "iBGP",
                  "200"
                ]
              ],
              "frame": "dashed"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Several protocols offer the same prefix: ==lower AD wins==",
                "==Metric== compares routes only {blue:within one protocol}",
                "RIP metric = **hop count**; OSPF metric = **cost** (from bandwidth)"
              ],
              "style": "check"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Never compare across protocols",
              "text": "AD picks the **source** first. A RIP metric of 2 does not beat an OSPF cost of 20; OSPF wins on ==AD 110 vs 120==."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Longest Prefix Match, Default Gateway and Static Routes",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 1,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Longest Prefix Match, Default Gateway and Static Routes",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Longest Prefix Match",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Of all routes containing the destination, the ==longest mask== (most specific) wins",
                "Wins {red:regardless of metric or order} in the table",
                "Default route `0.0.0.0/0` has prefix length 0: the ==last resort==, used only if nothing else matches"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Route",
                "Matches 10.1.1.5?",
                "Rank"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "`10.1.1.0/24`",
                  "yes",
                  "{green:1st: wins}"
                ],
                [
                  "`10.1.0.0/16`",
                  "yes",
                  "2nd"
                ],
                [
                  "`0.0.0.0/0`",
                  "yes (matches all)",
                  "last resort"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 0
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Default Gateway and Static Routes",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "==Default gateway== = router IP on the host's own subnet, used for any destination outside it",
                "It must be in the host's subnet, or traffic {red:never leaves}",
                "Static route: typed by hand, ==not adapted== if the link fails",
                "**Floating static**: set AD above the dynamic route (e.g. `200`) as a backup"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "bash",
              "lines": [
                "ip route add 10.2.0.0/16 via 192.168.1.1",
                "ip route 10.2.0.0 255.255.0.0 192.168.1.1"
              ],
              "note": "Linux (first), Cisco (second)"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Common Mistake",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "One-way route",
              "text": "Packets arrive but replies have ==no return route==. Always verify **both directions** and that the next hop is reachable."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Distance-Vector vs Link-State Routing",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 2,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Distance-Vector vs Link-State Routing",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Two Ways to Learn Routes",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "{blue:Distance-vector} (e.g. RIP): shares its ==whole table== with ==neighbours only==",
                "Learns the rest second-hand: **routing by rumour**",
                "{green:Link-state} (OSPF, IS-IS): **floods LSAs** to every router in the area",
                "Each router runs ==Dijkstra's SPF== on its own copy of the map"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "kind": "flow",
              "spec": {
                "nodes": [
                  {
                    "text": "Flood LSAs",
                    "ink": "blue"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Same LSDB",
                    "ink": "green"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Run SPF",
                    "ink": "purple"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Best paths",
                    "ink": "orange"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Comparison",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "Distance-vector",
                "Link-state"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Config",
                  "Simple",
                  "More design effort"
                ],
                [
                  "CPU / memory",
                  "Low",
                  "Higher"
                ],
                [
                  "Convergence",
                  "{red:Slow}",
                  "{green:Fast}"
                ],
                [
                  "Loops",
                  "{red:Prone}",
                  "Rare"
                ],
                [
                  "View",
                  "Neighbours' word",
                  "Full topology"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Distance-Vector Pitfalls",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "{red:Count-to-infinity} after a failure: stale routes bounce between neighbours",
                "==Split horizon==: never advertise a route back out the interface it came from",
                "==Poison reverse==: advertise it back as unreachable",
                "==Max hop limit==: RIP 15 is max, **16 = unreachable**"
              ],
              "style": "cross",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Path-vector (BGP)",
              "text": "A third type: each prefix carries its ==full AS path==. Seeing your own ASN means a loop, and policy can be applied."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "OSPF Basics",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 3,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "OSPF Basics",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. What OSPF Is",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "{blue:Link-state} IGP, IP protocol ==89==",
                "Multicast ==224.0.0.5== all routers, ==224.0.0.6== DR/BDR",
                "Cost = ==reference bandwidth / interface bandwidth==",
                "Default reference is 100 Mbps: raise it for **10G** links",
                "==Area 0== (backbone) is mandatory; other areas connect to it",
                "Areas limit LSA flooding and SPF scope; **ABRs** sit between areas"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Neighbours and DR",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "kind": "flow",
              "spec": {
                "nodes": [
                  {
                    "text": "Down",
                    "ink": "red"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Init",
                    "ink": "orange"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "2-Way",
                    "ink": "blue"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "ExStart",
                    "ink": "purple"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Exchange",
                    "ink": "purple"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Loading",
                    "ink": "blue"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Full",
                    "ink": "green"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Adjacency needs same ==area ID==, ==hello/dead== 10s/40s, subnet, authentication",
                "DR and BDR elected: highest ==priority==, then highest ==router ID==",
                "Election is ==non-preemptive==; DR cuts the number of adjacencies"
              ],
              "style": "check"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Cisco Example",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "cisco",
              "lines": [
                "router ospf 1",
                " network 10.0.0.0 0.0.0.255 area 0"
              ],
              "note": "enable OSPF on matching interfaces"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`show ip ospf neighbor` lists neighbours and states",
                "`show ip route ospf` shows learned routes marked ==O=="
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Stuck at Init or ExStart",
              "text": "Init: ==one-way hellos== (ACL, multicast). ExStart: ==MTU== mismatch."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "BGP Basics and Autonomous Systems",
